# =============================================================================
# nas.config — configuration for nas.sh
#
# THIS FILE IS THE SOURCE OF TRUTH. The copy nas.sh actually loads lives on the
# drive at /Volumes/Elements/nas.config; this is the versioned original, and the
# two are meant to be identical. After editing here:
#
#     cp NAS/pipeline/nas.config /Volumes/Elements/nas.config
#
# (and the reverse if you edited the drive copy in a hurry — but commit it).
# nas.sh prefers the drive copy and falls back to this one when the drive is not
# mounted; NAS_CONFIG=<path> overrides both.
# =============================================================================

# === Connection ===
HPC_USER=lkyamamo
HPC_HOST=endeavour.usc.edu
REMOTE_BASE=/scratch1/lkyamamo
LOCAL_BASE=/Volumes/Elements

# === Transfer mode default ===
# direct : recursive rsync with no compression (the only supported mode)
# Archive mode (HPC-side SLURM compression) was removed; see ARCHIVE_MODE_NOTES.md.
DEFAULT_MODE=direct

# === Symlinks (FIXED in nas.sh as of 2026-09-10) ===
# Previously: `rsync -az` implies -l, so symlinks were copied AS symlinks and never
# followed. The links in runs/ are absolute /scratch1/lkyamamo paths that do not
# exist on the Mac, so they arrived dangling — the file listing looked complete
# while the input it pointed at was not there (187 of 423 dangled).
#
# nas.sh now RELINKS them instead of dereferencing them. After each phase it walks
# the local tree, translates any absolute REMOTE_BASE/... target to the matching
# LOCAL_BASE path, and rewrites the link as a RELATIVE one. Costs no space, keeps
# the sharing intact, and survives the drive being renamed. Nothing in this file is
# needed for it; run it by hand with `nas.sh relink [--dry-run]`.
#
# --copy-unsafe-links was tried and rejected. rsync counts every absolute symlink as
# unsafe, so it replaces each with a full COPY of its target. Most of these links
# are small (potentials ~2 KB, start.data ~800 KB — all eight temperatures of run
# 0239 add about 6 MB), but some are DIRECTORY links into another run's output:
#     analysis/small_interface/voxel_0147/dumps -> runs/small_interface/0147/run/full
# is ~2.1 TB, copied a second time. It also destroys the sharing described under
# DELETION HAZARD below — six copies of final.data instead of six links to it.
#
# What relink cannot do: a link whose target is NOT under REMOTE_BASE has no local
# equivalent, so it is left alone and listed in
#     logs/unresolved_symlinks_<dirname>.tsv
# along with links that were rewritten but whose target has not arrived yet. Check
# that file after a sync rather than assuming every link resolves.
#
# DELETION HAZARD, unchanged: symlink targets are often OTHER RUNS' outputs —
# runs/0177/run/final.data is the start.data for six other runs. Pruning old runs
# by age or size will silently break newer runs' provenance. Check inbound links
# before deleting anything.

# === Rsync transfer priority ===
# Transfers run in two global phases:
#   PHASE 1 — for every directory: RSYNC_PRIORITY_FIRST, then everything
#             except RSYNC_PRIORITY_LAST
#   PHASE 2 — for every directory: RSYNC_PRIORITY_LAST
# so all the small/important files land before any bulk trajectory data starts.
# PHASE 1 is small, irreplaceable provenance: the inputs, the potential, the
# submit script, the initial and final configurations, and the run logs. The set
# below is ~everything you would need to explain or repeat a run.
#
# input_files/** must keep the ** — a bare "input_files/" matches the DIRECTORY,
# and since the pass already prepends --include="*/" every directory is included
# anyway, so the trailing-slash form is a no-op and files inside it fall through
# to the main pass. The ** is what actually pulls the contents. 187 input_files
# dirs, 10 MB total, so this is free. It also catches pot.mod and the *-therm.input
# variants that "in.*" misses (it anchors on the "in." prefix).
#
# vasprun.xml is VASP's complete machine-readable run record: the effective INCAR
# including every default VASP filled in, POTCAR identities, the k-mesh, and every
# ionic step. 170 files, 1.14 GB — 6% the size of OUTCAR for more of the
# reproducibility information. ICONST/HILLSPOT/REPORT are the constrained-MD and
# metadynamics inputs and history; all three together are under 15 MB.
#
# NOTE: *.data is here because start.data/final.data are structures, but it is not
# uniformly small — system_bubble.data, bubble_collapse.data and friends are 10 GB+
# each, roughly 60 GB of "phase 1". Deliberate, but it is not a small-files-only pass.
RSYNC_PRIORITY_FIRST=(
    "input_files/**"
    "in.*" "*.mod" "*.lammps" "log.*" "*.slurm"
    "POSCAR" "CONTCAR" "INCAR" "KPOINTS" "POTCAR" "OSZICAR" "vasprun.xml"
    "ICONST" "HILLSPOT" "REPORT"
    "*.data" "*.usc"
)

# PHASE 2 is bulk that can be re-fetched later, and that --skip-trajectory drops.
#
# OUTCAR was moved OUT of this list on purpose. It is not a trajectory — it is
# VASP's run log, the counterpart to log.lammps, which sits in phase 1. While it
# was here, any run with --skip-trajectory silently discarded 18 GB of VASP run
# logs. It now lands in the main pass: always transferred, never skipped. If that
# 18 GB is unwelcome, move it back knowingly rather than by accident.
#
# full/ hybrid/ int_dump/ look redundant against *.lammpstrj and nearly are. They
# earn their place by also catching rsync's own temp files (.full.2.lammpstrj.eRkTiC
# and similar), which do not end in .lammpstrj and would otherwise ride along in the
# main pass. full_dump/ and interface_dump/ must NOT be excluded as whole directories:
# they hold rdf_freud.py, rdf_freud_plot.py, run_dsf.sh, rdfs.csv and rdfs.png
# alongside the trajectories, hence the narrower full_dump/*.lammpstrj form.
#
# all_lammps.xyz is 231 GB in 12 files, 230 GB of it three concatenated dielectric
# trajectories. It is deferred rather than always-excluded because the other nine are
# ~0.12 GB structure-setup files in 1.setup_calc/ and 1.structure/.
# WAVECAR (72.77 GB) and CHGCAR (34.03 GB) are restart data: regenerable, but
# expensive to regenerate, so deferred rather than dropped. trajectory.h5 (57 GB) is
# a derived analysis product.
RSYNC_PRIORITY_LAST=(
    "*.lammpstrj" "*.dump"
    "full/" "hybrid/" "int_dump/" "full_dump/*.lammpstrj" "interface_dump/*.lammpstrj"
    "all_lammps.xyz" "trajectory.h5"
    "WAVECAR" "CHGCAR"
)

# === Never transfer ===
# Excluded from every pass, always, regardless of --skip-trajectory. Use this for
# data that is derived and disposable rather than data you might want later.
#
# dielectric.*.custom are the dielectric-production trajectories
# (dumps/dielectric.${i}.custom). They are reduced to dipole lines on the HPC and
# the sweep pipeline deletes them there once reduced, so there is no reason to
# pull them — and they are large enough to fill the drive on their own. Note they
# match neither *.lammpstrj nor *.dump, so without this entry they transfer in
# phase 1, before any deferred-file handling gets a say.
#
# CHG is VASP's low-precision charge density written alongside CHGCAR. CHGCAR is
# the one that can restart a calculation and is deliberately NOT excluded here;
# plain CHG is a visualization convenience and is by far the biggest single
# offender on the drive (546 GB across 62 files). Match is exact, so the glob
# must stay "CHG" and never "CHG*", which would swallow CHGCAR.
#
# lmp_mpi* are compiled LAMMPS binaries (ELF executables, ~106 MB each) that got
# copied into run directories alongside the data. 129 of them, 12.7 GB. They are
# build output, not results.
#
# dipole_rank_*_warn.log are the per-rank warning logs from the dipole reduction
# (198 files, 12.2 GB). The reduced products themselves — dipole_output.txt and
# dipole_output_timestep_data_cumulative.csv — are the point of that pipeline and
# are deliberately left in.
# dumps/ subsumes dielectric.*.custom (that entry is now shadowed and kept only as
# documentation). The directory holds nothing but dielectric.*.custom and two stray
# dump.N files, so pruning it whole is safe and saves rsync the descent.
#
# vasp_gam is the VASP executable itself — 109 copies, 1.88 GB — sitting in run
# directories exactly like the lmp_mpi binaries. Build output, not results.
ALWAYS_EXCLUDE=(
    "dielectric.*.custom"
    "dumps/"
    "CHG"
    "lmp_mpi*"
    "vasp_gam"
    "dipole_rank_*_warn.log"
)

# === Trajectory skipping ===
# When active, the RSYNC_PRIORITY_LAST globs above are not synced at all: phase 2
# is skipped entirely, and directories settle at MAIN_DONE rather than UPLOADED,
# so a later run without the flag picks them up and finishes them.
# Override per run with --skip-trajectory / --no-skip-trajectory.
SKIP_TRAJECTORY=false

# === Directories ===
# Format: "dirname" or "dirname:mode" where mode overrides DEFAULT_MODE
DIRECTORIES=(
    "analysis"
    "potentials"
    "runs"
    "structures"
)
